# Ad-Hoc Message Loop — Definition

The turn graph for `type: ticket` things: one overwritable thing per **work
item** — `ticket-<item>`: the change under review, the specification set,
the question — whose `status` is the addressee and whose body is the current
message (`standing-watch.md` → The Ticket). Never one per agent pair: a
pair's ticket carries unrelated items in turn and its history stops reading
as one conversation. Declare `ticket` in `_schema.yaml` with these actor
names as its statuses and `closed` as terminal; then each actor arms
`mdllm watch . --role <actor> --definition ad-hoc-message-loop --exit-on-wake`
the way its harness rises on an exit (`standing-watch.md` → Harness
bindings): in Claude Code as a background command, `/watch <actor>
ad-hoc-message-loop`; in Hermes as a notifying background terminal; never as
a blocking command inside a bounded tool call.

## The Rules

These are the whole contract. The first three also arrive with every wake,
because a rule that has to be looked up is the rule that gets skipped.

**Conversation**

1. A ticket is one conversation. Its status names whose turn it is. Its
   body is one message.
2. The wake brings the last few turns. Older history is `git log` on the
   file. Never copy a turn into the body.
3. Reply by replacing the body. Then hand the turn on, or close.
4. Anyone may leave a note on a ticket they do not hold: a short appended
   paragraph that says which kind it is — **steer** (act on this now),
   **follow up** (take it into your next turn) or **aware** (no action
   asked). The holder says in its next reply what it did with it.
5. A conversation has a budget of about twenty turns. At the budget,
   conclude it or split the work.

**Work**

6. The state of the work goes on the run and on the pull request. The
   ticket carries only what nothing else carries: never restate a state
   another record already holds.
7. A run's body says where the work is and what is next. Its past is git.
8. The run names who holds the work and who reviews it. The reviewer
   finds. The holder decides which findings to act on.

**Authority**

9. A go is the operator's own act: a signed commit from the operator's
   seat that names the commit it approves. Cite it. Never relay it, and
   never treat another seat's account of it as the go. *(Where a workspace
   has no signed operator seat yet, a go is the operator's own words in the
   session that acts on them.)*
10. A standing delegation is a written decision with a class of gate,
    conditions and an end date. Act inside it without asking. Outside it,
    address the operator's seat.

**Starting**

11. To start a loop: write the run with each role named to a seat, write
    each seat's opening ticket, launch any local seat that is not
    listening, take its first commit as the receipt, then act as a peer.
    Never send a message as the operator.
12. Who sits where is read from the commits. Nothing lists the seats.

**Stopping**

13. When blocked, address the ticket to the seat that can unblock you and
    say what you need. Never wait silently.
14. A doorbell that cannot read says so. Silence means nothing moved only
    when the bell can still look.

## Stages

**[actor-a]** / **[actor-b]**
- Entry: the other actor set `status` to this name and published.
- Performer: the named actor — the only writer of the turn while the ticket
  is at its name (rules 1 to 3). Another seat may leave a note (rule 4);
  the holder's watch rings on it.
- What it produces: one reply — replace the body, set `status` to the next
  addressee (or `closed`), commit, publish, and confirm the remote carries
  it. A second edit at the same status is a note, which rings the addressee
  and nobody else.
- Gate authority: none is conferred. A ticket carries requests, never
  approvals or rulings (rules 9 and 10); the recipient acts within its own
  permissions and the irreversible goes to the operator's seat.

**closed**
- Entry: whichever actor holds the turn judges that nothing is owed right
  now — the request was met, or the item moved on without one.
- What it means: nothing is owed. It is not the conversation's end; either
  party explicitly declares that end in committed state. The work item's
  completion does not substitute for an explicit conclusion.
  Anything durable the turn produced is a proper thing linked from the
  ticket. A later turn on the same item is a declared `closed → <actor>`
  move, which the addressee's watch sees.

The branch lifecycle that detects an explicit conclusion and stops its
watch is planned in `a2a-conversations-on-their-own-branch` at the framework
root. This turn graph alone does not implement that lifecycle or add a
conclusion status.

## Notes

The transcript is the ticket file's git history, and the wake hands over
its last three turns, so the body never has to hold them. Do not build
another message store. `mdllm validate` reports an open ticket whose body
holds more than one message; declare `options: {ticket_body: strict}` in
`_schema.yaml` to have that commit refused. Rename the actor ids to match the seats your domain's agents
actually sit in (`writer`, `reviewer`, or the agents' own names); each seat
declares itself in its clone with `mdllm seat . --set <actor>`. Two seats is
the default shape; a seat that only forwards another's actions (a publisher
reaching a service the other cannot) holds no stage here.
